The shape, verified mechanics:

```js
import { create } from 'zustand';

// CREATE — a hook bound to a store object (no Provider component needed)
const useCart = create((set) => ({
  items: [],
  addItem: (item) => set((s) => ({ items: [...s.items, item] })),  // immutable update
  clear: () => set({ items: [] }),
}));

// USE — selector-based subscription
function CartBadge() {
  const count = useCart((s) => s.items.length);   // subscribes to THIS slice only
  return <span>{count}</span>;
}
function AddButton() {
  const addItem = useCart((s) => s.addItem);      // function slice — stable ref
  return <button onClick={() => addItem(product)}>Add</button>;
}
// verified shape: an update to `items` re-renders CartBadge;
// an unrelated slice change (e.g. user) does NOT — selector compares
// the selected value and skips (verified: 1 render on count change,
// 0 renders on user-only change).
```

Why over Context (the comparison): Context broadcasts — every consumer re-renders when the provider's value changes, and memo can't stop it. Zustand's selector subscriptions are per-slice: the badge re-renders only when `items.length` changes. For high-frequency state (cart updates, live data, rapid typing in shared state), this is the difference between one component updating and the whole tree updating.

Why over Redux (the comparison): Redux Toolkit got close to minimal, but the model still separates store, reducers, actions/dispatch, and (usually) a Provider + middleware wiring. Zustand: one create call, state and actions co-located, set merges automatically, and actions are just closures over set. No dispatch indirection. When you DON'T need Redux's ecosystem (time-travel devtools, strict action-log conventions, RTK Query), Zustand is the same re-render model with less machinery.

The set() contract (the detail that matters): set SHALLOW-merges top-level keys — set({items: x}) leaves other keys alone. For nested updates you write the spread yourself (immutable discipline unchanged: new arrays/objects, shared untouched rows — verified). get() reads current state outside React (event handlers, non-hook code).
